1. d4 d5 2. c4 e5 3. dxe5 d4 { This is an interesting game where Lasker (White) does not target the advanced d4 pawn, making it the focal point of the game. Instead he plays around it. } 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. a3 Bg4 6. Nbd2 Qe7 7. h3 Bxf3 8. Nxf3 O-O-O { Alekhine could've won the pawn but he prefers to castle safely before he tries to win back his gambit pawn } 9. Qd3 { Not really a tactic but small combination that leaves Black in passive position if he takes the pawn right away. } 9... h6 (9... Nxe5? 10. Qf5+ Nd7 { At which point, White has more open game. } 11. Nxd4) (9... a6 10. Qf5+ Kb8 11. Ng5) 10. g3 g6 11. Bg2 Bg7 12. O-O Nxe5 13. Nxe5 Bxe5 14. b4 { White could've played Qe4 threatening mate but he activates more pieces first. } 14... f5 15. c5 { Idea is to play 16.c6 and threaten mate on b7 } 15... Qe6 16. c6 Ne7 (16... bxc6 17. Qa6+ Kb8?? (17... Kd7) 18. Bxc6) 17. cxb7+ Kb8 18. Bb2 Rd6 19. Rac1 Rhd8 20. Rc2 f4 21. gxf4 Bxf4 22. Rd1 { Combination alert!
Queen + Rook lined up against 2 Rooks. And the Rd1 is unprotected.
Rd1 & Rc2 can be forked by the Knight } 22... Nf5 23. Bc1 Ne3 24. Rc5 (24. fxe3 dxe3 25. Qxd6 Rxd6 26. Rxd6 Qxd6 $19) 24... Qf6 (24... Nxg2? { This does let Black win the pawn on b7 but I think White has the better game afterwards } 25. Kxg2 Bxc1 26. Rdxc1 R8d7 27. Qb5 (27. a4 Kxb7 28. Qf3+)) 25. Qe4 Nxd1 26. Bxf4 Nc3 27. Bxd6 Qxd6 (27... Nxe4?? 28. Bxc7+ Kxb7 29. Bxe4+ $18) 28. Qe5 Qb6 29. Qe7 Qd6 30. Re5 d3 31. exd3 Qxd3 (31... Qxe7 32. Rxe7 Rxd3) 32. Re3 Qd1+ 33. Kh2 Nb5 34. Re6 Nxa3 35. Rf6 { 1-0 White wins. } { Stockfish declares mate in 17 (at best) or mate is 7.
The main idea is to play Rf8 next move and Black really doesn't have any way to save his position
Rf6 seems pragmatic move as now Black has to be very careful to not get checkmated on back rank. } 35... Qd7 (35... Qd4 36. Rf8) (35... a6 36. Rf8 Rxf8 37. Qxf8+ Ka7 38. b8=Q#) (35... Nb5 36. Rf8) 36. Qxd7 Rxd7 37. Rf8+ Rd8 38. Rxd8# 1-0


